========================================================================
DATASET ATTRIBUTIONS AND PROVENANCE
========================================================================

1. Bitcoin Transaction & Network Data:
   This dataset suite contains transformed, derived, and modeled Bitcoin
   blockchain transaction graphs, transaction IDs (txids), addresses,
   fees, timestamps, and synthetic network observation layers.

   The base tables are published on Kaggle by Rosalin Nayak as
   "Bitcoin Transaction traffic":
   https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/rosalinnayak/bitcoin-transaction-traffic

2. Elliptic Data Set:
   This product includes data derived from the Elliptic Data Set
   (Elliptic, MIT-IBM Watson AI Lab), which provides labeled temporal
   graph transaction data for anti-money laundering (AML) detection.
   - Source: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/ellipticco/elliptic-data-set
   - Reference: Mark Weber et al., "Anti-Money Laundering in Bitcoin:
     Experimenting with Graph Convolutional Networks for Financial
     Forensics", KDD '19 Workshop on Anomaly Detection in Finance.
   - The original Elliptic dataset is subject to its respective upstream
     terms and research license.

3. IP Geolocation & Autonomous System Numbers (ASN):
   Includes IP geolocation data derived from DB-IP Lite (https://db-ip.com),
   licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International
   License (CC BY 4.0).
   The raw DB-IP databases are downloaded separately and not redistributed
   within this repository.

4. Bitcoin ETL:
   Transaction fields and schema (txid, inputs, outputs, addresses,
   values, fees, block timestamps) follow blockchain-etl/bitcoin-etl,
   MIT License: https://github.com/blockchain-etl/bitcoin-etl

5. Bitcoin transaction graph research dataset:
   Hugo Schnoering and Michalis Vazirgiannis, "Bitcoin Research with a
   Transaction Graph Dataset", arXiv:2411.10325, 2024, licensed under
   CC BY-SA 4.0: https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.10325

6. Synthetic & Challenge Metadata Layer:
   Generated via `ml/metadata_generator.py` and consolidated schemas,
   providing simulated P2P network traces, synthetic routing IPs,
   planted forensic patterns (CoinJoin, peeling chains, address reuse),
   and ground truth validation mappings.
